[twinkleplop-annotations]: https://twinkleplop.pngwn.at/docs/directives
[shiki]: https://shiki.style
# Syntax highlighting

The Vite plugin highlights fenced code and [inline code with a language](/docs/reference/highlighting#inline-code) with [twinkleplop][twinkleplop], with no setup. Every bundled language loads the first time a document has code.

Highlighted code is ordinary markup in your component, not `{@html}`. mdsvex escapes every `{` and `}` in it, so code is never read as a Svelte expression, whichever highlighter produced it.

::marks

## Themes

::::plate[A theme is a stylesheet](mark=".css", tone=other)
mdsvex adds no CSS. A twinkleplop theme is a stylesheet you import once, usually in your root layout:

```js
import '@twinkleplop/theme-github';
```

:::notes
The themes are `@twinkleplop/theme-github`, `theme-atom-one`, `theme-ayu`, `theme-catppuccin`, `theme-material`, `theme-night-owl`, `theme-rose-pine` and `theme-solarized`. Each has three stylesheets:

- the package itself, light colours by default and dark ones under a `.dark` ancestor, for sites that toggle a `dark` class;
- `/light` and `/dark`, one variant each, for a fixed mode.
:::
::::

::::plate[The reader's system setting](mark="dark", tone=other)
To follow the reader's system setting, import the light variant and add the dark one under a media query:

```svelte
<!-- src/routes/+layout.svelte -->
<script>
	import '@twinkleplop/theme-github/light';
	import '../app.css';

	let { children } = $props();
</script>

{@render children()}
```

```css
/* src/app.css */
@import '@twinkleplop/theme-github/dark' (prefers-color-scheme: dark);
```
::::

::::plate[A theme only colours tokens](mark="--twp", tone=other)
A theme only colours tokens. It sets `--twp-background` without applying it, and leaves the block's layout and the classes that [fence meta](/docs/reference/highlighting#fence-meta) and [code directives](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-directives) add to you:

```css
pre.twinkleplop {
	padding: 1em;
	overflow-x: auto;
	background: var(--twp-background);
}

.twinkleplop .highlight,
.twinkleplop .highlighted-word {
	background: rgb(127 127 127 / 0.2);
}

.twinkleplop .diff-add {
	background: rgb(0 200 0 / 0.15);
}

.twinkleplop .diff-del {
	background: rgb(200 0 0 / 0.15);
}

.twinkleplop .ln {
	display: inline-block;
	width: 2em;
	margin-right: 1em;
	text-align: right;
	opacity: 0.5;
}

.has-focus .l:not(.focus) {
	opacity: 0.5;
}
```
::::

::::plate[A highlighted block](mark="span.l", tone=fence)
A highlighted block renders as below. Each line is a `span.l`, each token a `span.tok` with its type as a class, and a line number a `span.ln`:

```html
<pre class="twinkleplop language-ts has-highlight" data-language="ts"><code><span class="l highlight"><span class="tok keyword">const</span> …</span></code></pre>
```

:::notes
On the atlas: [code fence](/atlas#code-fence).
:::
::::

## Highlight options

::::plate[`HighlightOptions`](mark="{}", tone=expr)
`highlight` takes these options in `mdsvex()`:

```ts
interface HighlightOptions {
	languages?: Record<string, LanguageModule | HtmlHighlighter | string>;
	default_language?: string;
	on_unknown_language?: 'plain' | 'throw';
	line_numbers?: boolean | { start?: number };
	annotations?: AnnotationPlugin[] | false;
	render?: RenderOptions;
	parse_meta?: (raw: string, parsed: RenderOptions) => RenderOptions | void;
	twoslash?: boolean | { svelte?: boolean };
}
```

:::notes
- `languages` adds languages and aliases, merged over the bundled ones. See [languages](/docs/reference/highlighting#languages).
- `default_language` highlights fences that name no language.
- `on_unknown_language` decides what happens to a language mdsvex doesn't have. `'plain'`, the default, renders it as plain code and warns once per file and language. `'throw'` fails the build. A language a plugin [claims](/docs/plugins/writing/bundles), such as `mermaid` with `@mdsvex/plugin-diagram`, is never looked up, so it neither warns nor throws.
- `line_numbers` numbers every fence's lines, `{ start: 10 }` from 10. A fence's own meta overrides it.
- `annotations` lists the [code directives](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-directives). Leave it out for the defaults, or pass `false` for none.
- `render` is twinkleplop's render options under every fence's own, such as `indent_guides`, `whitespace` or the `line` and `token` hooks. See [twinkleplop's render options](https://twinkleplop.pngwn.at/docs/render_options).
- `parse_meta` gets a fence's raw meta and the render options the [meta conventions](/docs/reference/highlighting#fence-meta) set, and returns the render options to use, for house conventions of your own.
- `twoslash` turns on [twoslash](/docs/reference/highlighting#twoslash).

`highlight` also takes a [function](/docs/reference/highlighting#custom-highlighters), or `false` to render every block as a plain `<pre><code class="language-js">`.
:::
::::

## Languages

::::plate[Bundled languages](mark="ts", tone=fence)
These languages are bundled: `bash`, `c`, `cpp`, `css`, `diff`, `dockerfile`, `dotenv`, `go`, `graphql`, `html`, `http`, `ini`, `javascript`, `json`, `jsonc`, `markdown`, `powershell`, `python`, `rust`, `shellsession`, `sql`, `svelte`, `toml`, `tsx`, `typescript` and `yaml`. Fences inside a `markdown` block are highlighted too.
::::

::::plate[Aliases](mark="js", tone=fence)
These aliases are built in:

| alias | language |
| --- | --- |
| `js`, `mjs`, `cjs` | `javascript` |
| `ts`, `mts`, `cts` | `typescript` |
| `jsx` | `tsx` |
| `sh`, `shell`, `zsh` | `bash` |
| `console`, `shell-session` | `shellsession` |
| `yml` | `yaml` |
| `md` | `markdown` |
| `env` | `dotenv` |
| `patch` | `diff` |
| `py` | `python` |
| `rs` | `rust` |

:::notes
A fence's language is looked up as written, then lowercased, so `Dockerfile` and `JSON` work.
:::

:::edges
- A language mdsvex doesn't have renders as plain code, in the same markup, so themes and line numbers still apply, with one warning per file. A fence with no language renders the same way, unless you set `default_language`.
:::
::::

::::plate[Languages of your own](mark="zig", tone=fence)
`languages` adds to these. A string is an alias. A twinkleplop language package is passed as its module namespace:

```js
import * as zig from '@acme/twinkleplop-zig';

mdsvex({
	highlight: {
		languages: { zig, zg: 'zig', svx: 'markdown', vue: 'html' }
	}
});
```

:::notes
A language can also be a function, `(code, render) => html`, that renders HTML itself.
:::

:::edges
- Code directives don't apply to it.
:::
::::

## Fence meta

The info string after the language takes the conventions of Shiki, VitePress and rehype-pretty-code:

::::added
:::add[`{1,3-4}`](mark="{1}", tone=fence, id=meta-lines)
highlights lines 1, 3 and 4 with the `highlight` class
:::

:::add[`{1,3-4}#id`](mark="{1}#", tone=fence, id=meta-line-id)
adds `data-highlighted-line-id="id"` to those lines
:::

:::add[`/word/`](mark="/w/", tone=fence, id=meta-word)
highlights every `word` with the `highlighted-word` class
:::

:::add[`/word/2-3`, `/word/#id`](mark="/w/2", tone=fence, id=meta-word-n)
the second and third `word` only, or adds `data-chars-id="id"`
:::

:::add[`:line-numbers`, `:line-numbers=10`, `:no-line-numbers`](mark=":ln", tone=fence, id=meta-line-numbers)
turns line numbers on, from 10, or off
:::

:::add[`showLineNumbers`, `showLineNumbers{10}`](mark="show", tone=fence, id=meta-show-line-numbers)
turns line numbers on, from 10
:::

:::add[`[math.ts]`, `title="math.ts"`](mark="[a]", tone=fence, id=meta-title)
a title
:::

:::add[`caption="…"`](mark="cap=", tone=fence, id=meta-caption)
a caption
:::

:::add[`twoslash`](mark="^?", tone=fence, id=meta-twoslash)
uses [twoslash](/docs/reference/highlighting#twoslash)
:::

:::add[`src=./math.ts`, `src=./math.ts#L5-L20`](mark="src=", tone=fence, id=meta-src)
shows [a file](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-from-a-file), or some of its lines
:::

:::add[`run`, `run=client`](mark="run", tone=fence, id=meta-run)
[runs the block](/docs/reference/code-that-runs) as a module, with the page or when asked
:::
::::

::::plate[A title and a caption](mark="title", tone=fence)
A fence with a title or caption renders inside a `<figure class="twinkleplop-block">`, with a `<figcaption class="twinkleplop-title">` above the code and a `<figcaption class="twinkleplop-caption">` below it:

````md score
```ts title="math.ts" {2} :line-numbers
export function add(a: number, b: number) {
	return a + b;
}
```
````

:::notes
A [replaced `pre`](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-block-components) gets them as props instead. With `highlight: false` there's no figure, and the fence renders as a plain `<pre><code>`.

The `language-` class carries only the language, never the rest of the meta.
:::

:::edges
- A line range past the end of the fence fails the build, so a stale range doesn't silently highlight nothing.
:::
::::

::::plate[`fence_meta`](mark="info", tone=expr)
A [parse plugin](/docs/plugins/writing/parse-plugins#parse_plugins) reads the same conventions with `fence_meta`, from the info string a `code_fence` node has in its handler:

```js
import { fence_meta } from 'mdsvex/compile';

const titled = {
	code_fence: {
		parse(node) {
			const { lang, title, src, props } = fence_meta(node.info);
			// 'ts', 'math.ts', undefined, [['wrap', true]] for ```ts [math.ts] wrap
		}
	}
};
```

:::notes
It gives the language, the rest as `meta`, the `title` and `caption`, what `src=` names, `run` for a fence that [runs](/docs/reference/code-that-runs), the `key=value` pairs and bare flags no convention claims as `props`, and every part as it was written as `parts`.
:::
::::

## Code from a file

::::plate[`src=`](mark="src=", tone=fence)
`src=` in the meta of a fence shows a file in place of code written in the document, so the docs never drift from the code they describe. The fence itself stays empty.

````md score
```ts src=./math.ts
```

```src=#lib/math.ts#L5-L20 title="math.ts"
```
````

:::notes
The path resolves as an import from the document would: a relative path, a Vite alias, or a `#lib/…` subpath import. Write `./math.ts`, since a bare `math.ts` is a package name to an import. Quote a path with spaces, `src="./my file.ts"`.
:::

:::edges
These fail the build, each at the fence:

- the file does not resolve or cannot be read;
- the slice asks for lines the file does not have, so a stale range never silently shows the wrong code;
- the fence has code of its own, which would otherwise go missing.
:::
::::

A slice after the path shows some of the lines, counted from 1:

::::added
:::add[`#L5`](mark="#L5", tone=fence, id=slice-line)
line 5
:::

:::add[`#L5-L20`](mark="L5-L20", tone=fence, id=slice-range)
lines 5 to 20
:::

:::add[`#L5-`](mark="#L5-", tone=fence, id=slice-to-end)
line 5 to the end
:::

:::add[`#-L20`](mark="#-L20", tone=fence, id=slice-from-start)
the start to line 20
:::
::::

::::plate[The language from the file name](mark=".ts", tone=fence)
A fence with no language takes it from the file name, so `src=./math.ts` is highlighted as `ts` and `src=./Dockerfile` as `dockerfile`. A language you write wins.

:::notes
Everything else in the meta works as it does on any fence: line highlights count the lines shown, a title is still `[math.ts]` or `title=`, and `src=` sets none.

The file is highlighted like code written in the fence, and a [replaced `pre`](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-block-components) gets `src` as a prop, as written. In dev, saving the file updates every page that shows it, and a watch build compiles those documents again.
:::
::::

::::plate[`sources`, for `compile()`](mark="{}", tone=expr)
`compile()` reads no files. Give it the text of each file in `sources`, keyed by the path as the fence has it, without the slice:

```js
compile(source, {
	sources: { './math.ts': 'export const a = 1;\n' }
});
```

:::notes
`sources: false` leaves `src=` unread, and the fence renders as written. The editor reads no files either, so it shows such a fence empty and reports only a fence that has code of its own.
:::

:::edges
- A fence whose file is not in `sources` throws a `SourceError`, which has a `code` (`src_missing`, `src_slice`, `src_body` or `src_spec`), the `spec`, and a `line` and `column`:

````md error
```ts src=./math.ts
```
````
:::
::::

## Code directives

::::plate[`[!verb]`](mark="[!hl]", tone=fence)
A code directive is a marker in a comment, `[!verb]`, that styles part of the code and disappears from the output. A line that holds only markers is dropped.

```js
const config = {
	theme: 'light', // [!del]
	theme: 'dark', // [!add]
	lines: true // [!hl]
};
```
::::

These are on by default:

::::added
:::add[`[!hl]`](mark="[!hl]", tone=fence, id=directive-hl)
`highlight`, a highlighted line
:::

:::add[`[!em]`](mark="[!em]", tone=fence, id=directive-em)
`emphasis`, drawing attention
:::

:::add[`[!focus]`](mark="focus", tone=fence, id=directive-focus)
`focus`, and `has-focus` on the `<pre>`, the lines to look at
:::

:::add[`[!dim]`](mark="[!dim]", tone=fence, id=directive-dim)
`subdued`, fading lines out
:::

:::add[`[!add]`, `[!del]`, `[!mod]`](mark="[!add]", tone=fence, id=directive-diff)
`diff-add`, `diff-del`, `diff-mod`, diffs
:::

:::add[`[!err]`, `[!warn]`, `[!info]`](mark="[!err]", tone=fence, id=directive-diagnostics)
`error`, `warning`, `info`, diagnostics
:::

:::add[`[!eval]`](mark="!eval", tone=fence, id=directive-eval)
no class, [live values](/docs/reference/live-code)
:::
::::

::::plate[Where a directive applies](mark="+2", tone=fence)
With no arguments, a directive applies to its own line. `[!hl +2]` applies to the two lines below the marker, `[!hl :3...5]` to lines 3 to 5, and `[!hl =total]` to every `total` on the marker's line.

:::notes
[Twinkleplop's directive docs][twinkleplop-annotations] have every form. The `<pre>` also gets a `has-` class for each class inside it, such as `has-diff-add`.
:::
::::

::::plate[`annotations`](mark="++", tone=fence)
`annotations` picks the directives. Content written for Shiki's transformers, `// [!code ++]`, works with `shiki_notation` added:

```js
import { default_annotations, shiki_notation } from 'mdsvex/highlight';

mdsvex({
	highlight: { annotations: [...default_annotations, shiki_notation()] }
});
```

:::notes
`annotations: false` turns directives off, and markers stay in the code as written.
:::
::::

## Inline code

::::plate[Inline code with a language](mark="#!ts", tone=code)
A code span that starts with ` #! ` and a language is highlighted:

```md score
Call `#!ts add(1, 2)` to add numbers.
```

:::notes
It renders as `<code class="twinkleplop twinkleplop-inline language-ts">`, with token spans inside and no ` #!ts `. A code span without ` #! ` stays a plain `<code>`. A [`code` replacement](/docs/guides/components#element-props) gets highlighted spans as `children`, and the language as a `lang` prop.

On the atlas: [code span](/atlas#code-span).
:::
::::

## Code block components

::::plate[A `pre` replacement](mark="<pre>", tone=comp)
A [`pre` replacement](/docs/guides/components) wraps the highlighted block. It gets these props:

| prop | value |
| --- | --- |
| `children` | the whole highlighted `<pre>` element |
| `lang` | the fence's language |
| `meta` | the rest of the info string, raw |
| `code` | the text a reader sees, without directive markers, with [live values](/docs/reference/live-code) in place |
| `title`, `caption` | from the [fence meta](/docs/reference/highlighting#fence-meta), when it has them |
| `src` | the path a fence [takes its code from](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-from-a-file), as written, slice included |
| `module` | the module of a fence marked [`run`](/docs/reference/code-that-runs) |
| `load` | a function that imports the module of a fence marked [`run=client`](/docs/reference/code-that-runs#running-on-the-client) |
| meta props | each part of the meta that no convention claims |

:::notes
`children` is the `<pre>` itself, not its `<code>`, because Svelte only keeps whitespace inside a `<pre>` it can see. A replaced `pre` gets no `<figure>`, so the title, caption and anything else around the code are up to the component.
:::

:::edges
- Render `children` with `{@render children()}` inside your own markup, never inside another `<pre>`.
:::
::::

::::plate[A copy button](mark="copy", tone=comp)
A copy button with a filename:

```svelte
<!-- src/lib/markdown/Pre.svelte -->
<script>
	let { code, title, children } = $props();

	let copied = $state(false);

	async function copy() {
		await navigator.clipboard.writeText(code);
		copied = true;
		setTimeout(() => (copied = false), 1500);
	}
</script>

<div class="code">
	<div class="bar">
		{#if title}<span>{title}</span>{/if}
		<button onclick={copy}>{copied ? 'copied' : 'copy'}</button>
	</div>
	{@render children()}
</div>
```

```js
// src/lib/markdown.js
export { default as pre } from './markdown/Pre.svelte';
```

:::notes
[`@mdsvex/plugin-code-blocks`](/docs/plugins/code-blocks) is a finished one, with an announced result and the caption, to use or to copy.
:::
::::

::::plate[Meta props](mark="key=", tone=fence)
Meta that no convention claims becomes props. `key="value"`, `key='value'` and `key=value` give a string, and a bare `key` gives `true`:

````md score
```svelte title="App.svelte" playground height=300
<h1>Hello</h1>
```
````

:::notes
That fence gives `playground={true}` and `height="300"`, as well as `title`. Meta props, `title` and `caption` don't depend on the highlighter, so a `pre` gets them with `highlight: false` too.
:::

:::edges
- Expression values, `key={…}`, aren't read.
- A meta prop named `lang`, `meta`, `code`, `title`, `caption`, `class`, `children`, `src`, `module` or `load` is dropped with a warning.
:::
::::

See the [code blocks playground example](/playground/code).

## Custom highlighters

::::plate[A highlighter function](mark="=>", tone=expr)
`highlight` can be a function instead of options. It gets the code and what mdsvex knows about it, and returns HTML:

```ts
type Highlighter = (
	code: string,
	info: { lang: string; meta: string; inline: boolean; filename?: string }
) => string | null | undefined;
```

:::notes
- `lang` is the fence's language, `""` when it names none, and `meta` the rest of the info string.
- `inline` is `true` for a [code span with ` #! `](/docs/reference/highlighting#inline-code). Plain code spans never reach the highlighter.
- A fence in a language a plugin [claims](/docs/plugins/writing/bundles) never reaches it either, the plugin renders that fence.
- It returns a whole `<pre>` for a fence, or a `<code>` for inline code. `null` or `undefined` renders that code plain.
:::

:::edges
- It must be synchronous. Do any async setup before `mdsvex()` is called, with top-level `await` in your Vite config.
- It returns ordinary HTML. Don't escape it for Svelte, with `escapeSvelte` or anything else, and don't wrap it in `{@html}`. mdsvex escapes every brace in what you return, so a `{@html …}` wrapper would show up as text.
:::
::::

::::plate[With Shiki](mark="shiki", tone=expr)
With [Shiki][shiki]:

```js
// vite.config.js
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { mdsvex } from 'mdsvex';
import { createHighlighter } from 'shiki';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

const shiki = await createHighlighter({
	themes: ['github-dark'],
	langs: ['javascript', 'typescript', 'svelte']
});

export default defineConfig({
	plugins: [
		mdsvex({
			highlight: (code, { lang, inline }) =>
				inline || !shiki.getLoadedLanguages().includes(lang)
					? null
					: shiki.codeToHtml(code, { lang, theme: 'github-dark' })
		}),
		sveltekit()
	]
});
```

:::notes
Shiki's transformers go in the `codeToHtml` call, as they would anywhere else. A [`pre` replacement](/docs/reference/highlighting#code-block-components) wraps a custom highlighter's output too, and gets the same props.
:::
::::

## Twoslash

::::plate[Twoslash](mark="^?", tone=fence)
Twoslash shows type information from the TypeScript compiler in your code. It is opt-in, as it starts a TypeScript environment. Install it:

```bash
npm i --save-dev @twinkleplop/twoslash
```

:::edges
- Turning it on without the package installed is an error that names the package.
:::
::::

::::plate[Turning twoslash on](mark="twosl", tone=fence)
Turn it on, and import its styles next to your theme:

```js
mdsvex({ highlight: { twoslash: true } });
```

```js
import '@twinkleplop/twoslash/style.css';
```

````md score
```ts twoslash
const total = [1, 2, 3].reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0);
//    ^?
```
````

:::notes
Fences with `twoslash` in their meta then go through twoslash. `twoslash: true` covers `ts`, `js` and `tsx` fences. For Svelte fences too, install `@twinkleplop/twoslash-svelte` as well and use `twoslash: { svelte: true }`.
:::
::::

## Highlighting with `compile`

::::plate[`create_highlight`](mark="create", tone=expr)
`compile` is synchronous and loads nothing, so it doesn't highlight unless you pass `highlight`. `mdsvex/highlight` builds the same configuration the plugin uses:

```js
import { compile } from 'mdsvex/compile';
import { create_highlight, load_default_languages } from 'mdsvex/highlight';

const highlight = create_highlight({
	languages: await load_default_languages()
});

const { code } = compile(source, { highlight, filename: 'post.svx' });
```

:::notes
`create_highlight` takes the [options](/docs/reference/highlighting#highlight-options) above, apart from `twoslash`, which takes highlighters by language. `load_default_languages` loads every bundled language once.
:::
::::

::::plate[`language_loaders`](mark="await", tone=expr)
In a browser, `language_loaders` loads them one at a time, so you only fetch what you use:

```js
import { create_highlight, language_loaders } from 'mdsvex/highlight';

const highlight = create_highlight({
	languages: {
		svelte: await language_loaders.svelte(),
		typescript: await language_loaders.typescript()
	}
});
```

:::notes
`compile` also takes a [highlighter function](/docs/reference/highlighting#custom-highlighters), or `false`.
:::
::::
